# AI Runtime Project Rules

## Goal

This project focuses on building a reliable AI runtime, not a feature-rich application.

Always prefer simplicity over abstraction.

Avoid generating unnecessary code.

---

# Architecture

Always follow this architecture.

HTTP Request

↓

Controller

↓

Service

↓

Repository

↓

TypeORM

↓

MySQL

Controllers never access the database.

Controllers never contain business logic.

Services never contain SQL.

Repositories contain all database operations.

---

# Backend Folder Structure

Each business module lives under

modules/

Example

modules/runtime

runtime.controller.ts

runtime.service.ts

runtime.module.ts

runtime.dto.ts

runtime.types.ts

Persistence lives under

repositories/

Each entity gets its own folder.

Example

repositories/runtime

runtime.entity.ts

runtime.repository.ts

runtime.repository.module.ts

Never create a shared entities folder.

---

# Repository Rules

Every repository owns exactly one entity.

Repositories should expose reusable methods.

Use TypeORM Repository API.

Avoid QueryBuilder unless absolutely necessary.

---

# Frontend Rules

Frontend is intentionally simple.

Do not introduce Redux.

Do not introduce Zustand.

Do not introduce React Query.

Use

- React
- Axios
- useState
- useEffect

Only.

Every API call must go through

services/

Never call Axios directly inside components.

---

# Component Rules

Components should stay under approximately 200 lines.

Extract reusable UI.

Prefer composition.

---

# DTO Rules

Every endpoint must validate using DTOs.

Never use any.

---

# API Responses

Always return

{
  success,
  data,
  message
}

---

# Styling

Use Shadcn defaults.

Avoid unnecessary custom CSS.

---

# Error Handling

Backend should throw HttpExceptions.

Frontend should display user-friendly errors.

---

# Database

Use MySQL.

Use TypeORM.

Use snake_case table names.

Use UUID primary keys unless otherwise specified.

---

# Code Style

Prefer early returns.

Prefer small functions.

Avoid deeply nested if statements.

Write readable code over clever code.

---

# Comments

Do not add comments explaining obvious code.

Only document non-trivial business decisions.

---

# Scope

Do not add extra features unless explicitly requested.

Always optimize for reviewability.

Favor depth over breadth.
